            {
                "drawing_id": "7bb60657-eed8-4929-95e4-8b4b2e5f9386",
                "locked": false,
                "rotation": 0,
                "svg": "<svg width=\"36\" height=\"24\"><text font-family=\"TypeWriter\" font-size=\"10.0\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#000000\" fill-opacity=\"1.0\">.130</text></svg>",
                "x": -867,
                "y": -186,
                "z": 2
            },
            {
                "drawing_id": "7ba05e66-a058-4b77-949c-bd1a3c2b02c1",
                "locked": false,
                "rotation": 0,
                "svg": "<svg width=\"596\" height=\"280\"><text font-family=\"TypeWriter\" font-size=\"10.0\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#000000\" fill-opacity=\"1.0\">All devices are preconfigured :\n   - Host are in the correct VLANS\n   - SW1-SW2 are connect via trunk\n   - R1 -SW2 are connected via ROAS\n\n1.Replace the ROAS configuration on R1 and SW2 with a point to point Layer 3 connection\n   Use the IP addresses give in the diagram\n   Configure a default route on SW2 with R1's G0/0 interface as the next hop\n\n2.Configure SVIs on SW2 one for each VLAN \n   Assigh the last usable IP address of each subnet to the appropriate SVI\n\n3.Test inter-VLAN connectivity by pinging betweenVLANs\n\n4.Test connectivity to the internet by pinging 1.1.1.1\n  (Routes have already been configured on R1 and the Internet router)\n</text></svg>",
                "x": -936,
                "y": 214,
                "z": 2
            },
